 Use the provided spreadsheet tools to complete the requested workbook. Preserve source inputs.
+
+## Period alignment
+
+The period header row is the authority for which period a column belongs to.
+Each rate, price, margin or growth figure in a column applies to THAT column's
+period and to no other column. Never compensate for spending an anchor opener by
+shifting a later column's rate into an earlier column.
+
+Anchor and predecessor rules:
+
+- A forecast that builds from a historical or opening figure starts in the FIRST
+  displayed forecast period and applies that period's own rate.
+- Only genuinely later periods chain from their own immediately preceding
+  forecast period.
+- Do not leave a required forecast cell blank to make room for a later period.
+
+Fill every cell the task asks for. Cells the task calls intentionally blank stay
+blank; nothing else should be blank.
+
+## Normalizing reported results
+
+Normalization rebuilds a period's result as if the non-recurring items had not
+occurred. It is not a weighted-average or a re-weighting of the reported base.
+
+Work out the direction of each line item from what it did to the reported figure:
+
+- A non-recurring EXPENSE reduced the reported result, so removing it ADDS its
+  full amount back to the pre-tax base.
+- A non-recurring GAIN raised the reported result, so removing it SUBTRACTS its
+  full amount from the pre-tax base.
+
+Use the item's own full amount, not the amount multiplied by a rate. The tax rate
+associated with an item governs only the tax effect, never the base movement.
+
+## Tax effect per adjustment
+
+Each adjustment's tax effect is computed separately, on that adjustment's own
+amount, at that adjustment's own rate:
+
+- Removing an expense restores the tax it sheltered: reported tax PLUS
+  expense_amount * expense_rate.
+- Removing a gain removes the tax it attracted: reported tax MINUS
+  gain_amount * gain_rate.
+
+Reported tax already embeds the tax on the reported result. Add the separate
+adjustment effects to it; never recompute the whole tax charge from a blended
+rate and never apply one adjustment's rate to another adjustment or to the
+adjusted pre-tax total.
+
+## Consistency check
+
+Derived rows should equal their own definitions: net profit = pre-tax minus tax;
+a margin divides the matching numerator by the matching period's revenue. If an
+adjustment's tax effect and its base movement have opposite signs of benefit, one
+direction is inverted; re-derive both before delivering.
+
+## Building the workbook
+
+Put real Excel formulas in the cells, referencing the source cells, so an
+independent engine can recalculate them. Do not paste precomputed numbers.
+Reference the row holding a true constant (an opening or historical value, a
+fixed rate) with absolute markers where a formula needs it, and chain forecast
+periods with ordinary relative references so the arithmetic reads period by
+period.
+
+Use the workbook tools to inspect the source and write the requested output path
+as a copy of the source, changing only the cells the task asks for. Leave
+original inputs, labels, units and intentionally blank cells exactly as they are,
+and do not add, delete or rename rows, columns, sheets or labels. Read the cell
+comments first, since they carry conventions the labels leave ambiguous.
